  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(-1)+{f}^{\u2032}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(-1)}{2}(x+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(-1).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at $x=-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(-1)=9{e}^{3(-1)}=9{e}^{-3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(-1)}{2} & =\\frac{9{e}^{-3}}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
